import { CorrectionCopy } from "../../correction-model"

pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Map names a lookup table with chosen key and value types. A lookup returns a value that also represents a missing key.",
  taskTitle: "Look up a price and keep the earlier price table",
  purpose: "Use Map to associate a product name with its price. Map<String, Int> is the type of a table with string keys and integer values. Updating a price leaves the earlier table available, and a missing product stays distinct from a product priced at zero.",
  setup: "import * as maps from \"std/map\" makes the Map module available under the name maps. maps.Map names its type; names such as maps.fromEntries name operations. let prices: maps.Map<String, Int> = ... names a value prices and checks it against the type after the colon. String means text and Int means an integer. <String, Int> chooses the key type first and the value type second.",
  codeTitle: "Build a price table, retain an update, and read a missing product",
  outputTitle: "Seseragi output",
  after: [
    "[(\"tea\", 0), (\"coffee\", 5), (\"tea\", 2)] is an Array containing three pairs. Parentheses and a comma in (\"tea\", 0) group two values, with the key first and its price second. fromEntries builds a Map from these pairs. The repeated tea key takes its last price, 2, but keeps its first position. entries converts the Map back to an Array of pairs, so the first output shows tea then coffee, priced at 2 and 5.",
    "maps.insert \"tea\" 3 prices supplies three arguments: the key, the price, and the original table. Spaces separate arguments in a call. Naming the result updated gives a table whose tea price is 3. prices remains usable with tea priced at 2. Parentheses around an inner call group its result as one argument to the outer call.",
    "fn priceLabel prices: maps.Map<String, Int> -> String = ... declares a function. The name follows fn; prices names its argument and the colon introduces that argument's type. -> String says the function returns text. The expression after = supplies the returned value. This local prices is whichever table the caller supplies.",
    "maps.get \"tea\" prices returns one of two shapes: Just price when the key exists, or Nothing when it does not. Just wraps the found value; Nothing represents absence. The type containing these two possibilities is named Maybe. match selects the branch whose shape fits the returned value. Nothing -> \"not listed\" returns the missing-product label. Just price -> show price names the found value price and turns it into text. Both branches return text, which explains priceLabel's String return type.",
    "singleton \"tea\" 0 creates a one-product table. Its lookup returns Just 0, so the third line is 0. empty () creates an empty table; its lookup returns Nothing, producing not listed on the fourth line. () is the sole value of the Unit type and carries no additional information. Here priceLabel's argument type tells the compiler that the empty table is also a Map<String, Int>. There is no truthiness test that might mistake zero for absence.",
    "The final == compares whether the same keys have the same values. Building the table in coffee, tea order still gives the same associations, so the result is true. Map preserves insertion order when reading entries, but ignores that order when comparing table contents."
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "Use TypeScript Map for the same price table",
  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript's Map<string, number> also chooses key and value types in that order. new Map builds the table from pairs, and get reads a price. new Map(prices).set(\"tea\", 3) first copies the table and then updates the copy, preserving prices. Ordinary Map already makes this a short task. [...prices] spreads its entries into an array, and JSON.stringify turns them into display text. every checks that all pairs satisfy the condition; ([key, value]) names each pair's key and price. && requires both the size check and the price checks to succeed.",
  comparisonAfter: "TypeScript get returns undefined for a missing key, so priceLabel checks specifically for undefined and preserves zero. const prevents rebinding the name, not changes to Map contents. Seseragi insert returns a new table without changing the earlier one. The TypeScript example compares sizes and checks each key's price; === between two JavaScript Map objects would not compare their contents. Both examples use the same string keys and small integers; TypeScript number is not identical to the whole Seseragi Int type. TypeScript prints pairs as JSON and Seseragi uses show, so punctuation and spacing differ.",
  detailTitle: "What Map checks, and a common type mix-up",
  details: [
    "Map<String, Int> says every key is a String and every value is an Int. An empty table has this type too. The two _ positions in the exact declaration Map<_, _> stand for the two types you choose; they are not example arguments to pass literally. The label opaque-type means the internal representation is private. Public operations such as fromEntries, singleton, and empty still construct values.",
    "let prices: maps.Map<String, Int> = { tea: 2 } produces SES-T0101 because it assigns an ordinary record to a Map type. { tea: 2 } is a different kind of value with a field named tea. An annotation does not convert it. Construct the Map with maps.fromEntries [(\"tea\", 2)] or maps.singleton \"tea\" 2. TypeScript Map<string, number> likewise does not describe an ordinary { tea: 2 } object.",
    "Key lookup needs equality and hashing support, recorded as Eq and Hash requirements. Equality decides whether two keys match; hashing calculates a value used to locate candidates. String provides both. Comparing Map contents also requires comparing their values; the Int prices here support that. An empty constraints list on the type declaration does not remove the requirements of individual operations.",
    "The keys in this comparison are strings. JavaScript's identity rules for object keys do not model every possible Seseragi key type. The shared name Map also does not promise identical JavaScript performance. Lookup and update costs follow the selected operation and the key's equality and hashing rules."
  ]
}
